<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>France 24 - International breaking news, top stories and headlines</title><subtitle type="html">Breaking news and world news from France 24 on Business, Sports, Culture. Video news. News from the US, Europe, Asia Pacific, Africa, Middle East, America</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/rss</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>France: Watching the Post-Riot Protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b49ec496d5bb4401036a32ca986ece6bd0ea0272263f16aee0e2cca9d4b57ac3</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T18:42:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261024-france-watching-the-post-riot-protests" /><summary type="html">After the French protests turned into riots last weekend, the curfews, and then the alleged racist mass shooting yesterday on the outskirts of Paris, there has been much anxiety, planning and preparation ahead of today's protests.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>“Witkoff should step down” says Ukraine Committee Chief</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:332d4e34d33577c1cff25b56adbe77b82ef293333b1a0974d47aa42e6e09029d</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T18:37:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261024-witkoff-should-step-down-says-ukraine-committee-chief" /><summary type="html">Ukraine’s access to Starlink and Starshield satellite links has been restored to the speeds it had before restrictions that disrupted front-line operations for more than a week, Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said Saturday.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>Thousands take to Tel Aviv's streets for protests against Netanyahu</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fde8a543efef0262321c7f576dff7284293da8926a84537b7dc5db1b5f51d24a</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T18:21:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261024-thousands-take-to-tel-aviv-streets-for-protests-against-netanyahu" /><summary type="html">Thousands marched against Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's government in Tel Aviv on Saturday – just three days before Israel's general election – demanding accountability for the failure to prevent a Hamas-led attack on October 7, 2023, that led to the war in Gaza.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Yemen's Houthi rebels claim new attacks on oil facilities and airports across Saudi Arabia</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7ae842fc17b73834914667fe29c68fcd35798c25bde2eb488f408f9a37ddcde4</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T17:50:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261024-yemen-s-houthi-rebels-claim-new-attacks-on-oil-facilities-and-airports-across-saudi-arabia" /><summary type="html">Yemen’s Iran-allied Houthi rebels said they launched attacks at Saudi oil facilities and airports on Saturday while Yemen’s Saudi-backed government ​said the ‌group also fired three ballistic missiles at coast guard sites in ⁠the port city of Aden.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>France: Clashes erupt as protests sweep across cities</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fdf1914d43cfde0733139c317448fb04aaf57d8fe2f42244c87056a1d6a4069d</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T17:50:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261024-france-clashes-erupt-as-protests-sweep-across-cities" /><summary type="html">🇫🇷 The Ministry of the Interior estimates at least 410,000 people rallied across France for Saturday’s protests against the high cost of living, including 110,000 in Paris. 
 
Meanwhile, reported nearly 800,000 demonstrators and 250,000 in the capital. 
 
The situation in Paris remained largely "calm" during the day, while clashes broke out in other cities following attempts to enter unauthorised areas.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israel begins demolitions in West Bank</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c72bdaa74dd274d7f6ee71a803c06358b8e72ac90f3e7b6543bbfa8a2c0f0f58</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T17:13:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/israel-begins-demolitions-in-west-bank" /><summary type="html">The Palestinian Authority has called for international pressure on Israel after a Palestinian official said around 1,200 Palestinians in Hebron in the West Bank were forced to leave their homes following an attack on Friday which killed two Israeli soldiers. The head of Hebron's municipality said an additional 1,300 may also be forced to leave.</summary></entry><entry><title>Italy hit by new cost of living protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0c9553d3af97927bc0f72d19b3ebdf21a21bef833300dec826ee01261ee910f8</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T17:12:54Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/italy-hit-by-new-cost-of-living-protests" /><summary type="html">Soldiers were deployed across Milan and luxury boutiques boarded up as protesters took to the streets again on Saturday, a week after looting and clashes with police. Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni announced a series of measures, including sending an additional 500 soldiers to the city and introducing tougher penalties for minors, who police said made up a large proportion of last week’s violent protesters. Surveillance helicopters circled central Piazza del Duomo, while soldiers patrolled in groups alongside military vehicles and a water cannon. Some visitors said they would leave the city centre if violence broke out again. Our Rome correspondent, Seema Gupta, has the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>Paris: 70.000 police officers deployed nationwide</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5bb91ccb0a525c14fed42c207b87c6a4021251720eb64c732cf672b9e65bf708</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T17:11:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/paris-70-000-police-officers-deployed-nationwide" /><summary type="html">A coalition of the “Yellow Vests” movement, trade unions and students will march across France on Saturday with demands over studying conditions and purchasing power. Some 70,000 police are expected to be deployed as authorities fear looting and vandalism similar to last Saturday's unrest.</summary></entry><entry><title>France: Protests continue while police fire tear gas at demonstrators</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:851b32d27f87db4fedd2941686ccc636260e99eec970af89a06f1e4201c2a67b</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T17:10:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-protests-continue-while-police-fire-tear-gas-at-demonstrators" /><summary type="html">A total of 410,000 people took part in demonstrations across France on Saturday – including 110,000 in Paris – as part of a day of rallies focussed on purchasing power and the defence of public services, the Interior Ministry told AFP. The turnout was slightly lower than last week, which saw 550,000 people take to the streets.

Meanwhile, according to the union responsible for organising the authorised events, 800,000 people participated in the demonstrations nationwide on Saturday, with 250,000 in the capital.

Clashes broke out in several cities, including Paris, Lyon, Rennes, Nantes, Bordeaux, Nice, and Marseille. Matthew Mary-Caruchet shares further details.</summary></entry><entry><title>🔴 French protests live: Police say 110,000 turn out for 'calm' Paris march amid reports of clashes in other cities</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:80e333d3798c5f7d411ae3ec382a2add1fda098ec3a3cc02127596143d7e9098</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T15:42:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261024-live-hundreds-of-protests-expected-in-france-amid-high-tensions-and-heavy-police-presence--fda62057d1da.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261024-live-hundreds-of-protests-expected-in-france-amid-high-tensions-and-heavy-police-presence" /></entry><entry><title>🔴 French protests live: Police fire teargas at demonstrators in cities including Toulouse, Lyon</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4bdf1c9e65a3841a4454468f0f54ba25df1d861487f36c50c8f272c7040f6a78</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T14:41:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261024-live-hundreds-of-protests-expected-in-france-amid-high-tensions-and-heavy-police-presence--fda62057d1da.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261024-live-hundreds-of-protests-expected-in-france-amid-high-tensions-and-heavy-police-presence" /></entry><entry><title>NFL's Paris Premier: Saints RB Alvin Kamara on global reach, sport as catalyst for youth opportunity</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5d742fd358b4d5ce5cc8837344666e9284510e8da5f811d0e663efe8e03b3415</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T14:20:03Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261024-nfl-s-paris-premier-saints-rb-alvin-kamara-on-global-reach-sport-as-catalyst-for-youth-opportunity" /><summary type="html">François Picard and Selina Sykes are pleased to welcome New Orleans Saints running back Alvin Kamara. As the National Football League expands its international ambitions to Paris, FRANCE, Kamara offers a unique perspective that reaches well beyond the spectacle of American football's arrival in the French capital. Just days before the Saints' anticipated encounter with the Pittsburgh Steelers, Kamara describes the occasion as "an opportunity unlike any other," while reflecting on his remarkable career, his interest in representing his country in flag football at the Los Angeles 2028 Olympics, and his ambitions to develop sporting opportunities in Liberia, his parents' country of birth.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>NFL's Paris Premier: Saints RB Alvin Kamara on global reach, sport as catalyst for youth opportunity</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:11a7189c77adb55ac7175d8a7ee39560317e526bf023462d2955c349b10669cc</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T14:15:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261024-nfl-s-paris-premier-saints-rb-alvin-kamara-on-global-reach-sport-as-catalyst-for-youth-opportunity-1" /><summary type="html">François Picard and Selina Sykes are pleased to welcome New Orleans Saints running back Alvin Kamara. As the National Football League expands its international ambitions to Paris, FRANCE, Kamara offers a unique perspective that reaches well beyond the spectacle of American football's arrival in the French capital. Just days before the Saints' anticipated encounter with the Pittsburgh Steelers, Kamara describes the occasion as "an opportunity unlike any other," while reflecting on his remarkable career, his interest in representing his country in flag football at the Los Angeles 2028 Olympics, and his ambitions to develop sporting opportunities in Liberia, his parents' country of birth.</summary></entry><entry><title>Israeli forces displace 1,200 Palestinians in Hebron after deadly attack on soldiers</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:164c32047c34ba4b8f9ac6c0edbfcc235bbb0335a45da7055828621eaa5ee513</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T13:50:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261024-israeli-forces-displace-1-200-palestinians-in-hebron-after-deadly-attack-on-soldiers" /><summary type="html">The Palestinian Authority has called for international pressure on Israel after a Palestinian official said around 1,200 Palestinians in Hebron in the West Bank were forced to leave their homes following an attack on Friday which killed two Israeli soldiers. The head of Hebron's municipality said an additional 1,300 may also be forced to leave.</summary><category term="Middle East" /></entry><entry><title>Fresh protests across France</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3a986dd8193e02c2825a8d4f96adc149dc923e1165c2b1b32902605f101c3ddf</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T13:22:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/fresh-protests-across-france" /><summary type="html">A coalition of the “Yellow Vests” movement, trade unions and students will march across France on Saturday with demands over studying conditions and purchasing power. Some 70,000 police are expected to be deployed as authorities fear looting and vandalism similar to last Saturday's unrest.</summary></entry><entry><title>Macron condemns racist terrorist attack in Paris suburb</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:715b23e64969daa30c9dc04efec737bdc41322d7f6cf306ae88eda4655de1acf</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T13:21:02Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/macron-condemns-racist-terrorist-attack-in-paris-suburb" /><summary type="html">Adding to the tensions, France is reeling from a shooting that authorities are investigating as a potentially racist and Islamophobic terrorist attack. One person was killed and five others wounded in the Paris suburbs of Trappes and La Verrière, including a 12-year-old girl who was shot eight times. Police arrested the suspected gunman, and investigators reportedly found three gas cylinders rigged to a timer in his apartment, as well as a submachine gun in his car. The French president has condemned the attack, while the mayor of Saint-Denis has denounced the impact of hate speech.</summary></entry><entry><title>France: Anti-far-right protest in Orleans</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:36590d341d40b46af70b164b7d3e9421ffb32d32f7ad0c1c8f146709d7bcccf9</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T13:19:54Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-anti-far-right-protest-in-orleans" /><summary type="html">Far-right candidate Marine Le Pen is officially launching her presidential campaign this weekend at the National Rally’s two-day party congress in Orléans, where thousands of supporters are expected to attend. Local authorities have put exceptional security measures in place as anti-far-right demonstrations take place in the city.</summary></entry><entry><title>Paris: Protest starts at Porte d'Italie, after last-minute change to the route</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0ee632c3d7d675577f4e20dd82e38f5513938c790473ffceae6a873b305b0718</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T13:18:52Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/paris-protest-starts-at-porte-d-italie-after-last-minute-change-to-the-route" /><summary type="html">France is bracing for another day of cost-of-living protests, with extra police deployed nationwide to prevent a repeat of the violence that followed last Saturday’s demonstrations. In Lyon, where protests have been banned, police used tear gas to disperse around 150 people who had gathered to demonstrate. Government figures put last weekend’s turnout at more than half a million, and similarly large crowds could take to the streets again today. The movement grew out of high school students’ protests over poor conditions and a lack of resources, and cities across the country have stepped up security. In Paris, the Louvre, Orsay and Picasso museums are closed, while many luxury boutiques have also shut their doors and boarded up their windows following last weekend’s looting. France 24 Journalist Elitsa Gadeva reports the latest.</summary></entry><entry><title>Andrew Smith warns that policing, political blame are no substitute for social reform &amp; public trust</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:bbf718b6d980c964f1bafa9a0ec64563d2e3efb5ad00516bc09bc844e763f6e4</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T13:09:03Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/andrew-smith-warns-that-policing-political-blame-are-no-substitute-for-social-reform-public-trust" /><summary type="html">“You can't police yourself into social cohesion. You can't police yourself into a political future,” explains Andrew Smith, historian of modern France at Queen Mary University of London. He identifies a central paradox confronting the French Republic: the state may possess the means to impose public order, yet lack the political capacity to address the demands and grievances driving mass mobilisation. Smith examines the tensions between social protest, presidential authority and democratic legitimacy amid renewed unrest in France: “500,000 people on the streets of France doesn't happen by chance.”</summary></entry><entry><title>France: Thousands march in Orléans against National Rally congress</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f4143236f292cdb12885b671c40d3fd4f25e03c119385d11e53f4fa36f4b2b05</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T11:45:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261024-france-thousands-march-in-orl_ans-against-national-rally-congress--f231df26b1ee.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261024-france-thousands-march-in-orl%C3%A9ans-against-national-rally-congress" /><summary type="html">Thousands of anti-fascist protesters took to the streets of Orléans on Saturday to demonstrate against a congress held by Marine Le Pen’s far-right National Rally party. Marchers criticised both Le Pen and President Emmanuel Macron, chanting slogans and carrying banners opposing the far right. The protest came amid renewed nationwide demonstrations over the cost of living, a week after rallies descended into street violence. The two-day congress also marked a major campaign event for Le Pen ahead of France’s presidential election in six months’ time. Antonia Kerrigan reports the latest from Orleans.</summary></entry><entry><title>US seeks to topple Cuba's government</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e3974b2eac28ee1ed58ff96c14b7f370d104a28903d73c1f842b7e14ff71490d</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T11:43:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/us-seeks-to-topple-cuba-s-government--b52eb58eab6b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/us-seeks-to-topple-cuba-s-government" /><summary type="html">And now, in a rather surprising development, US Secretary of State Marco Rubio is directly urging Cubans to cooperate with the CIA. Havana has responded by mocking what it calls the Trump administration’s “desperation”.</summary></entry><entry><title>Donald Trump challenges Alaska Senate trace</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:98f0f6a086b42d3587494102a2e2b2a8cdbe6313930f5c4c27f79a832c519219</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T11:42:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/donald-trump-challenges-alaska-senate-trace--8639a06bdbc9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/donald-trump-challenges-alaska-senate-trace" /><summary type="html">With the US midterm elections less than two weeks away, concerns are growing over whether President Donald Trump would accept a major Republican defeat. Trump has called for legal action to block the certification of Alaska’s Senate race if Republican incumbent Dan Sullivan loses to his Democratic challenger, Mary Peltola, arguing that another candidate with the same name on the ballot could confuse voters. Our Washington correspondent Fraser Jackson has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>France: Young demonstrators demand concrete action from Macron</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b73b32f3e8a85d5fb3cd673cae15fa492a93d901739a628d3f2dc4c59f45bc2d</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T11:41:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-young-demonstrators-demand-concrete-action-from-macron--d1843bce28f5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-young-demonstrators-demand-concrete-action-from-macron" /><summary type="html">Student protests in France began more than a month ago, over poor conditions in high schools, teacher shortages, the pace of the school day and inequalities between private and public education. The movement has since grown into a broader mobilisation, bringing together the Yellow Vests, trade unions and wider civil society. Joseph Downing, Senior lecturer in politics and international relations and Aston University, shares further details,</summary></entry><entry><title>Trump administration sues to block possible Democratic win in Alaska Senate race</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:67beebaed056e68ead9fd7ecc6d745ae8397242c54037bba253854bd5189f265</id><updated>2026-10-24T18:51:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-24T11:40:50Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/americas/20261024-trump-administration-sues-to-block-possible-democratic-win-in-alaska-senate-race--2cdc4eae1c83.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261024-trump-administration-sues-to-block-possible-democratic-win-in-alaska-senate-race" /><summary type="html">US President Donald Trump said Friday that he had instructed his administration to file a lawsuit to block the certification of the US Senate race in Alaska if Republican Senator Dan Sullivan loses, arguing that voters would be confused because two Republicans on the ballot have similar names. The legal battle is ramping up questions over whether Trump will accept a potential midterms defeat.</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry></feed>
